    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A good treadmill warm-up takes five to ten minutes and simply builds from an easy walk to
          the effort of your main session, a little at a time. A good cool-down does the same in
          reverse. Neither needs to be elaborate, and the treadmill makes both easier than they are
          outdoors — you can raise and lower the speed in exact steps, and the slow start is built
          into getting on the belt safely anyway.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Warm up for 5 to 10 minutes.</strong> Start with an easy walk on a flat or
            near-flat deck, then raise the speed (and, if your session uses it, the incline) in
            small steps every minute or two until you are close to the pace of the main session.
            Take the longer end of that range before running, hard intervals or steep hill work,
            and the shorter end before an easy walk.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cool down for 5 to 10 minutes.</strong> Bring the incline back to flat first,
            then lower the speed to an easy walk and stay there until your breathing has settled
            and you could hold a normal conversation. Then stop the belt and step off carefully.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you feel dizzy afterwards,</strong> hold the handrails, step your feet onto
            the side rails, and sit down until it passes. A brief "the floor is moving" feeling is
            common and harmless; feeling faint is not something to walk off. The
            <a href="#dizziness" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dizziness section</a> below goes
            through it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest of this page explains why those steps matter, how to set each one on a
            treadmill, and the few drills worth doing on the floor beside the machine before a
            harder session. The step-on and step-off basics are covered in our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-use-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to use a treadmill</a>,
            and this page builds on them rather than repeating them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-warm-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What a Warm-Up Is Actually For</h2>
<p>
            The honest case for a warm-up is less dramatic than it is sometimes made to sound, and
            more convincing for being modest.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It lets your heart rate and breathing rise gradually.</strong> Going from
            standing still to a hard effort in thirty seconds is uncomfortable for almost everyone:
            breathing races ahead of you, the first few minutes feel far harder than they should,
            and many people conclude they are having a bad day. Building into it over several
            minutes gives the cardiovascular system time to catch up, and the main session usually
            feels noticeably easier as a result.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It warms the working muscles.</strong> Muscles and connective tissue behave
            better when they are warm, and gentle movement is how you warm them. That is why a few
            minutes of walking does more than standing still and stretching.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It rehearses the movement.</strong> Easy walking and jogging let you settle into
            the belt, find your position on the deck and get your stride sorted before the speed
            leaves any margin for error.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It works as a check.</strong> The warm-up is where you find out that your calf
            is tight, your shoe is rubbing or you are more tired than you thought. Better to learn
            that at 3 mph than at 7.
          </p>
          <p>
            What a warm-up does not reliably do is guarantee you against injury. Research on
            warm-ups and injury prevention is mixed and depends heavily on the sport and the type
            of warm-up studied. We would not oversell it on those grounds. The reasons above are
            enough on their own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How Long Should a Treadmill Warm-Up Be?</h2>
<p>
            The rule of thumb is simple: the harder the main session, the longer the warm-up.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Easy walk:</strong> 3 to 5 minutes. Start slower than your walking pace and
            build to it. For a gentle walk, the warm-up and the session blur together, and that is
            fine.</li>
            <li><strong>Incline walk:</strong> 5 minutes on the flat or at 1 to 2%, then raise the
            grade a percent or two at a time rather than in one jump.</li>
            <li><strong>Easy run:</strong> 5 to 10 minutes — a few minutes of brisk walking, then a
            few minutes of very easy jogging, before you settle at your running pace.</li>
            <li><strong>Intervals, tempo runs or hill repeats:</strong> 10 to 15 minutes, finishing
            with a few short pickups (described below) so the first hard rep is not the first time
            your legs have moved quickly.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Two factors stretch the warm-up further. <strong>Time of day:</strong> most people feel
            stiffer first thing in the morning and benefit from a slower start. <strong>Age and
            history:</strong> older runners and walkers, and anyone returning from a layoff or
            injury, often find a longer, gentler warm-up makes the whole session more comfortable.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most treadmill built-in programs include a warm-up phase, and they are often only two
            or three minutes long. There is nothing wrong with using them — just start the program
            after you have already walked for a few minutes, or extend the phase if your console
            allows it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do the warm-up minutes count toward your session? For general health targets, yes:
            easy walking is still walking. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long to walk on a treadmill</a>
            covers weekly totals. For a structured run, it is usual to treat the warm-up and
            cool-down as separate from the "main set", so the session is not quietly shortened.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="treadmill-warm-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. A Treadmill Warm-Up, Step by Step</h2>
<p>
            This is a sensible default before a run or a harder session. Shorten it for an easy
            walk; lengthen the final stage before intervals.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Minutes 0 to 2 — easy walk.</strong> Start the belt at its lowest speed,
            straddling the side rails, and step on once it is moving. Build to an easy walking
            pace, somewhere around 2.5 to 3 mph for most adults. Flat deck. Let go of the rails
            once you are steady.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Minutes 2 to 4 — brisk walk.</strong> Raise the speed in small steps to a brisk
            walk, roughly 3.2 to 3.8 mph. If the main session uses incline, add 1 to 2% here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Minutes 4 to 7 — easy jog (runners only).</strong> Raise the speed to a very
            easy jog — slower than your usual easy pace. Bring the grade back to around 1% if you
            were walking on an incline. Shoulders loose, eyes ahead.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Minutes 7 to 10 — settle in.</strong> Build to your normal easy running pace,
            or to the steady pace of your session. By the end you should be breathing a little
            harder, warm, and comfortable.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Before hard work, add pickups.</strong> Three or four 20-second pickups at
            roughly the pace of your hard reps, with a minute of easy jogging between them. Raise
            the speed using the arrow buttons rather than jumping straight there with a quick key,
            and come back down the same way. Pickups prime the legs for faster running without
            tiring them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Throughout: change one setting at a time, and change incline before speed when going
            up. Our <a href="/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pace and speed chart</a>
            converts the mph on the display into minutes per mile if you think in outdoor paces.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dynamic-drills" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Dynamic Drills — Off the Belt</h2>
<p>
            Before a hard run, many runners add a few minutes of dynamic movement — controlled,
            rhythmic movements through a range of motion — after the walking part of the warm-up.
            They are optional for an easy walk and useful before intervals, hill repeats or a race
            effort.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do them on the floor beside the treadmill, not on the belt.</strong> Lunges,
            skips, side-steps and high knees on a moving belt are an excellent way to end up in a
            heap at the back of the deck. Stop the belt, step off, do the drills, then step back on
            and restart from a slow speed. It costs a minute and removes the risk entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            A short routine of five to eight minutes is plenty:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Leg swings</strong> — holding the treadmill frame or a wall, swing each
            leg forward and back ten times, then side to side across the body ten times.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking lunges</strong> — six to eight per leg, upright through the
            torso.</li>
            <li><strong>Calf raises</strong> — ten to fifteen slow raises on both feet, or on a
            step. Particularly useful before hill sessions.</li>
            <li><strong>Hip circles or "open the gate"</strong> — lift a knee and rotate it out to
            the side, five or six per leg.</li>
            <li><strong>High knees and butt kicks on the spot</strong> — fifteen to twenty seconds
            each, light and quick.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Long static stretches — holding a stretch for thirty seconds or more — are better kept
            for after the session, or for a separate time. Holding long stretches immediately
            before a fast effort is not harmful for most people, but it does not warm you up, and
            some evidence suggests it can briefly reduce power output. Moving does the warming.
          </p>
          <p>
            If floor space beside your treadmill is tight, our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space requirements guide</a>
            covers the clearances. Do not use the clear zone behind the deck for drills while
            anyone else is on the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-cool-down" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Why Cool Down at All?</h2>
<p>
            The cool-down has one clear, practical job and several claimed jobs that are less well
            supported.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The clear job: a gradual return to rest.</strong> During running, your leg
            muscles help push blood back toward the heart with every stride. Stop suddenly after a
            hard effort and that pumping stops, while blood vessels in the legs are still wide
            open. In some people, blood pressure dips enough to cause lightheadedness — the
            classic "I stopped and the room spun" moment. A few minutes of easy walking keeps the
            legs working while your heart rate and breathing come down, and that is the most
            convincing reason to cool down.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The treadmill-specific job: stepping off safely.</strong> The cool-down doubles
            as the safe stopping sequence. Walking slowly on a flat deck is the right state to be
            in when you stop the belt, and if you are lightheaded you want that to happen while
            you are still holding on, not as you step down.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The weaker claims.</strong> Cooling down is often said to prevent next-day
            muscle soreness or "flush out lactic acid". The evidence for a cool-down reducing
            soreness is weak, and lactate clears from the blood on its own within an hour or so
            whether you walk or sit. A cool-down is worth doing — just for the reasons above rather
            than those.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="treadmill-cool-down" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. A Treadmill Cool-Down, Step by Step</h2>
<p>
            Five to ten minutes, in this order:
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>1. Bring the incline down first.</strong> If you have been on a grade, lower it
            to flat or 1% while you are still moving at your session pace or just below it. Many
            machines return the deck to level when you press stop, but it is better to arrive at
            the stop already flat.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>2. Lower the speed in steps.</strong> From a run, drop to an easy jog for a
            couple of minutes, then to a brisk walk, then to an easy walk. From a hard walk, drop
            to a comfortable walk and then an easy one. Use the arrow buttons rather than a quick
            key that drops you straight to a crawl.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>3. Walk until you can talk.</strong> The finish line is when your breathing has
            settled and you could hold a normal conversation, not a fixed number of minutes. After
            an easy session that may take three minutes; after hard intervals or a long run in a
            warm room it may take ten.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>4. Stop the belt and keep walking as it slows.</strong> Then step your feet out
            onto the side rails, hold the handrails, and step off only when the belt has stopped
            completely — to the side or the back, carefully, without turning around on the deck.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>5. Stand still for a moment.</strong> Before you walk away, stand beside the
            machine for a few seconds. If anything feels off, sit down.
          </p>
          <p>
            After the cool-down is a good time for a few minutes of gentle static stretching if you
            enjoy it — calves, hamstrings, hip flexors and quadriceps are the usual list.
            Stretching after a session feels good for many people and does no harm, though it is
            not a reliable way to prevent soreness or injury.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dizziness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Feeling Dizzy After Running on a Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Feeling odd after stepping off a treadmill is common, and there are two quite
            different sensations that get described as "dizziness". It helps to tell them apart.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The floor feels like it is moving.</strong> After twenty minutes or more on a
            belt, your brain has adjusted to the ground moving under you. When you step onto solid
            floor, it briefly expects the same, and you may feel as if you are lurching forward or
            the room is sliding. This is a perceptual after-effect, it is harmless, and it usually
            passes within a minute or so. Holding the rail as you step down and standing still for
            a moment is all it needs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lightheaded or faint.</strong> This is different: a woozy, greying-out or
            about-to-faint feeling, sometimes with nausea. The most common reasons after exercise
            are:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Stopping abruptly</strong> from a hard effort, so blood pools in the legs —
            the reason the cool-down exists.</li>
            <li><strong>Heat.</strong> A warm room with no airflow makes your body work much harder
            to shed heat, and treadmill runners often overheat without noticing.</li>
            <li><strong>Dehydration</strong>, particularly on long or hot sessions.</li>
            <li><strong>Not having eaten</strong> for a long time beforehand.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>What to do in the moment.</strong> Do not try to walk it off or stride away
            from the machine. Hold the handrails, step your feet onto the side rails, and press
            stop or pull the safety key. Once the belt has stopped, sit down — on the floor if
            necessary — and stay seated until the feeling has passed completely. If you feel faint,
            lying down with your legs raised is the usual first-aid position. Get up slowly, sip
            some water, and do not get back on the belt for the rest of that session.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Preventing it.</strong> Always cool down gradually, put a fan on yourself and
            keep the room cool, drink during long sessions, and avoid hard sessions on an empty
            stomach if you know you are prone to it. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-running-outside/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs running outside</a>
            page explains why heat is the hidden variable indoors.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>When it is a medical question.</strong> If you actually faint, if dizziness comes
            with chest pain, a racing or irregular heartbeat or unusual breathlessness, if it
            happens during exercise rather than just after, or if it keeps happening despite a
            proper cool-down, stop exercising and see a doctor. Those are not cool-down problems,
            and a review site is not the place to work out the cause.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="programs-and-consoles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Built-In Programs, Auto Cool-Downs and the Stop Button</h2>
<p>
            Consoles handle the ends of a session in different ways, and it is worth knowing how
            yours behaves before you rely on it. Your manual is the authority for your model.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Program warm-ups are often short.</strong> Two or three minutes at a fixed low
            speed is common. That is fine for a walk; for a run, either walk for a few minutes
            before starting the program or treat the program's warm-up as the second half of
            yours.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Auto cool-downs vary.</strong> Some programs finish with a cool-down phase that
            lowers speed and incline over a few minutes; others simply stop. A few consoles enter a
            cool-down mode when you press stop once, and stop fully on a second press. If you are
            not sure what yours does, find out at walking speed rather than at the end of a hard
            run.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The stop button is not a cool-down.</strong> Pressing stop from a fast pace
            brings the belt to rest over a few seconds, which is too quick for your circulation
            and awkward for your feet. Use it at the end of the cool-down, at walking pace.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Safety key, always.</strong> Clip it on before you start the warm-up, not when
            you reach the "real" session. People stumble at slow speeds too, especially while
            reaching for a bottle or changing settings. The key should stay clipped to you until
            the belt has stopped.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-session-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Matching the Bookends to the Session</h2>
<p>
            A quick reference for the most common session types. All figures are typical starting
            points.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Easy walk (20 to 45 minutes):</strong> 3 to 5 minutes building from a slow walk
            to your walking pace; 3 to 5 minutes easing back down at the end. No drills needed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline walk, including the
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30</a>:</strong>
            5 minutes flat, then raise the grade a percent or two at a time to the working grade.
            At the end, step the grade down before the speed, then walk flat for 3 to 5 minutes.
            Calf raises beforehand are a sensible addition.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Easy run:</strong> 5 to 10 minutes from walking to easy jogging; 5 minutes of
            easy jogging and walking at the end.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Intervals or hill repeats:</strong> 10 to 15 minutes including pickups, with
            dynamic drills off the belt if you like them; 5 to 10 minutes of easy jogging and
            walking at the end. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-interval-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval guide</a>
            and <a href="/treadmill-hill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">hill workout guide</a>
            have the main sets.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Long run:</strong> the first mile or two at a deliberately easy pace is the
            warm-up; 5 to 10 minutes of easy walking at the end, with water, is the cool-down. Long
            runs are the sessions most likely to leave you lightheaded, so take the cool-down
            seriously.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. The Short Version</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Warm up for 5 to 10 minutes and cool down for 5 to 10 minutes.</strong> Build
            from an easy walk to the effort of your session in small steps, and come back down the
            same way — incline first, then speed — until you can talk comfortably.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before hard sessions, add a few short pickups on the belt and any dynamic drills on the
            floor beside it, never on the moving belt. Keep long static stretches for afterwards.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you feel the floor moving when you step off, hold the rail and stand still for a
            moment. If you feel faint, stop the belt, step onto the side rails and sit down — and if
            it comes with chest pain, palpitations or actual fainting, that is a question for a
            doctor.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a treadmill warm up be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Five to ten minutes for most sessions: three to five minutes before an easy walk, and ten to fifteen before intervals, tempo runs or hill repeats. Start with an easy walk on a flat deck and raise the speed in small steps every minute or two until you are close to the pace of your main session.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you cool down on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Lower the incline to flat first, then reduce the speed in steps — from a run to a jog, then a brisk walk, then an easy walk — over five to ten minutes. Keep walking until your breathing settles and you could hold a conversation, then stop the belt, step onto the side rails and step off once it has stopped.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do I feel dizzy after running on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually one of two things. A brief sense that the floor is moving is a harmless after-effect of the belt and passes within a minute. Feeling lightheaded or faint is more often from stopping abruptly, heat, dehydration or not having eaten. Sit down until it passes; if you faint or it comes with chest pain or palpitations, see a doctor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I stretch before running on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Moving is a better warm-up than holding stretches. Walk and jog first, then add dynamic drills such as leg swings, lunges and calf raises — on the floor beside the treadmill, never on the moving belt. Long static stretches are better kept for after the session if you enjoy them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to stop running on a treadmill suddenly?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is not dangerous for most people, but it is a common cause of lightheadedness, because blood pools in the legs when they stop pumping. It is also awkward on a belt. Slow to a walk and keep walking for a few minutes before pressing stop, and hold the rails if you need to stop quickly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the warm-up count as part of my treadmill workout?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For general activity totals, yes — easy walking is still walking. For a structured session such as intervals or a tempo run, it is usual to count the warm-up and cool-down separately from the main set so the hard part is not quietly shortened.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Treadmill Warm-Ups and Cool-Downs</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">None of these are dramatic, which is why they persist. The second and third are the ones that cause falls.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Jumping straight to session pace</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Using a quick key to go from standing to a run makes the first ten minutes feel far harder than they should and leaves no time to find your position on the belt. Build up in small steps with the arrow buttons over five to ten minutes instead.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doing drills on the moving belt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Lunges, side-steps, skips and high knees on a moving belt are a straightforward route to a fall. Stop the belt, step off, do the drills on the floor beside the machine, then step back on and restart from a slow speed.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressing stop at full speed and stepping off</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Stopping from a hard effort is the commonest cause of post-run lightheadedness, and stepping off a decelerating belt is the commonest way people fall. Walk for several minutes at the end, then stop the belt and step off only once it is still.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Walking off dizziness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">If you feel faint after a session, the instinct is to stride away from the machine. Sit down instead and stay down until it passes completely. Fainting, chest pain, palpitations or dizziness during exercise are reasons to see a doctor rather than to tweak your cool-down.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

